== Adoratio Crucis | The Adoration of the Cross
R: Dum dicuntur Orationes, duo Sacerdotes se induant in sacristia alba, et cingulo, cum stolis et manipulis nigris, sine casulis, pro cantandis Versibus, et tenenda Cruce in altari. Item etiam se præparent duo Diaconi, sine dalmaticis, ut cantent *Agios o Theós,* ad gradum presbyterii, ante Crucis adorationem. Completis Orationibus, Prior deposita casula (in sacristia reponenda) ab altari discedat, et descendens cum suis ministris gradus altaris ad extremum angulum in cornu Epistolæ, stent omnes in presbyterio ab eadem parte altaris, versis vultibus ad partem Evangelii : ita ut unus sit ad latus alterius; et propius altare stet primo Sacerdos, deinde alii, eo ordine, quo solent in presbyterio sedere. Tunc duo Sacerdotes destinati ad tenendam Crucem, venientes ad gradus altaris, genuflectant : deinde surgentes accipiant Crucem, altari superpositam, et velatam, et stantes juxta extremam partem altaris in latere Epistolæ, ac tenentes Crucem reverenter per brachia, unus ad dexteram, et alter ad sinistram, versi ad Conventum, vel populum, cantent
 = While the Orations are said, two priests vest in the sacristy in alb and girdle, with black stoles and maniples, without chasubles, to sing the Verses and hold the Cross at the altar. Two deacons also make ready, without dalmatics, to sing the *Agios o Theós* at the step of the presbytery before the adoration of the Cross. When the Orations are finished, the Prior, having laid aside the chasuble (to be put away in the sacristy), leaves the altar and goes down the steps with his ministers to the far corner on the Epistle side; all stand in the presbytery on that side of the altar, facing the Gospel side, one beside another, the Priest standing nearest the altar, then the others in the order in which they usually sit in the presbytery. Then the two priests appointed to hold the Cross come to the steps of the altar and genuflect; then rising they take the veiled Cross placed on the altar and, standing near the end of the altar on the Epistle side and holding the Cross reverently by the arms, one on the right and the other on the left, facing the Community or the people, they sing:
T: {℣.} Pópule meus, quid feci tibi, aut in quo contristávi te? respónde mihi. Quia edúxi te de terra Ægýpti, parásti Crucem Salvatóri tuo.
 = {℣.} My people, what have I done to you? or in what have I grieved you? Answer Me. Because I led you out of the land of Egypt, you have prepared a cross for your Saviour.
C: ad 0
R: Quo finito, duo Diaconi ad gradum presbyterii stantes respondeant
 = When this is finished, the two deacons standing at the step of the presbytery answer:
T: Agios o Theós, Agios ischyrós, Agios athánatos, eléison imas.
 = Holy God, holy and strong, holy and immortal, have mercy on us.
R: Dum pronuntiant *Agios,* flectant genua : dum *o Theós,* surgant. Secundo flectendo genua, dicant *Agios,* et surgendo, *ischyrós.* Tertio flectant genua dicendo *Agios,* et surgendo, dicant *athánatos, eléison imas.*
 = As they say *Agios* they kneel; at *o Theós* they rise. Kneeling a second time they say *Agios,* and rising, *ischyrós.* Kneeling a third time they say *Agios,* and rising they say *athánatos, eléison imas.*
R: Deinde Fratres in choro respondentes *Sanctus,* genua flectant : subdendo *Deus,* surgant. Iterum flectendo genua, dicant *Sanctus,* et surgendo, *fortis.* Tum tertio flectant genua, dicentes *Sanctus,* et surgendo adjungant, *et immortális, miserére nobis.*
 = Then the brethren in choir, answering *Sanctus,* kneel; adding *Deus,* they rise. Kneeling again they say *Sanctus,* and rising, *fortis.* Then kneeling a third time they say *Sanctus,* and rising they add *et immortális, miserére nobis.*
R: Prior vero, et ministri, et Presbyteri qui Crucem tenent, non flectant genua. Sed neque prædicti Diaconi cum Conventu, neque Conventus cum ipsis genua flectat.
 = But the Prior, the ministers and the priests holding the Cross do not kneel; nor do the said deacons kneel with the Community, nor the Community with them.
R: Postquam sic Chorus responderit, Sacerdotes Crucem tenentes, uno, aut altero passu procedant, recedendo a latere Epistolæ versus medium altaris, et ibi sistendo cantent secundum
 = When the Choir has answered thus, the priests holding the Cross move one or two paces, withdrawing from the Epistle side towards the middle of the altar, and stopping there sing the second
T: {℣.} Quia edúxi te per desértum quadragínta annis, et manna cibávi te, et introdúxi in terram satis óptimam, parásti Crucem Salvatóri tuo.
 = {℣.} Because I led you through the desert forty years, and fed you with manna, and brought you into a land exceeding good, you have prepared a cross for your Saviour.
R: Quo finito, Diaconi secunda vice cantent *Agios o Theós, etc.* et post illos Chorus similiter respondeat *Sanctus Deus, etc.* genua flectendo, et surgendo, sicut fecerunt prima vice. Tunc Sacerdotes cum Cruce procedentes, atque ad medium altaris descendentes gradus, sistant ubi Crux deponenda erit pro adoratione, et cantent tertium
 = When it is finished, the deacons sing *Agios o Theós, etc.* a second time, and after them the Choir likewise answers *Sanctus Deus, etc.,* kneeling and rising as they did the first time. Then the priests, going forward with the Cross and coming down the steps at the middle of the altar, stop where the Cross is to be laid for the adoration, and sing the third
T: {℣.} Quid ultra débui fácere tibi, et non feci? Ego quidem plantávi te víneam meam speciosíssimam, et tu facta es mihi nimis amára : acéto namque mixto cum felle sitim meam potásti, et láncea perforásti latus Salvatóri tuo.
 = {℣.} What more ought I to have done for you, and have not done? I planted you, My most beautiful vineyard, and you have become exceeding bitter to Me; for in My thirst you gave Me vinegar mixed with gall to drink, and with a lance you pierced the side of your Saviour.
R: Subjungant tandem Diaconi *Agios o Theós, etc.* et Chorus similiter respondeat *Sanctus Deus, etc.* et genua flectant, ac surgant tam Diaconi, quam Chorus, sicut prima, et secunda vice fecerunt. Quibus cantatis, Prior veniat ad medium ante altare, et se collocet in loco aliquantulum superiore, medium inter duos Sacerdotes, qui per brachia Crucem sustinentes, et suos vultus ad se invicem vertentes, tenebunt Crucem ante Priorem : ministri vero discedant a cornu altaris, dum discedit Prior, et se collocent unus post alium in eadem parte presbyterii, versis vultibus ut antea ad partem Evangelii, omnesque stent eo ordine, quo prius stabant. Tunc Prior ex manibus duorum Sacerdotum Crucem velatam accipiet, et iisdem adjuvantibus, reverenter eam deteget, detectamque populo ostendet : eamque ambabus manibus elevando, incipiet Antiphonam
 = Finally the deacons add *Agios o Theós, etc.,* and the Choir likewise answers *Sanctus Deus, etc.;* and both deacons and Choir kneel and rise as they did the first and second times. When these have been sung, the Prior comes to the middle before the altar and places himself a little higher, midway between the two priests, who, holding the Cross by the arms and facing each other, hold it before the Prior; the ministers leave the corner of the altar as the Prior leaves it and place themselves one behind another on the same side of the presbytery, facing the Gospel side as before, all standing in the order in which they stood before. Then the Prior takes the veiled Cross from the hands of the two priests and, with their help, reverently uncovers it and shows it uncovered to the people; and raising it with both hands he begins the Antiphon:
T: Ecce lignum Crucis, in quo salus mundi pepéndit : veníte adorémus.
 = Behold the wood of the Cross, on which hung the salvation of the world: come, let us adore.
C: ad 1
R: Hac eo incipiente, tam Chorus, quam ministri altaris, Sacerdotes, et Diaconi exsistentes in suis locis flectant genua, inclinantes profunde : et prosequente Cantore Antiphonam, dum dicit *Crucis,* surgant omnes, Choro Antiphonam continuante. Quamdiu cantatur, Prior semper elevatam Crucem sustineat, et ostendat ut supra. Finita Antiphona, Crucem restituat in manus prædictorum duorum Sacerdotum, qui eam deponent, et reclinabunt ad gradus presbyterii super tapetem ibidem stratum, et seipsos collocabunt hinc et inde ad duo latera Crucis, accumbentes super latera sua juxta Crucem, et versis ad se invicem vultibus, brachia Crucis quilibet a sua parte tenendo.
 = As he begins it, the Choir and the ministers of the altar, the priests and the deacons, kneel where they are, bowing profoundly; and as the Cantor continues the Antiphon, at the word *Crucis* all rise, the Choir continuing the Antiphon. As long as it is sung, the Prior keeps the Cross raised and shows it as above. When the Antiphon is finished, he gives the Cross back into the hands of the two priests, who lay it down and rest it at the steps of the presbytery on a carpet spread there, and place themselves on either side of the Cross, reclining on their sides beside it and facing each other, each holding the arm of the Cross on his side.
R: Tunc fiat ab omnibus ministris, et Fratribus publica sanctæ Crucis adoratio; in cujus ritu, seu ordine adorandi, inter adorantes et adoraturos servetur ordinata accedendi distantia, hoc modo : a loco, ubi Crux adoranda jacet, deorsum versus ecclesiæ navim sumatur spatium quindecim circiter passuum, (prout loci conditio, situsque permiserit) et ab ultima hujus spatii extremitate primo Prior, nudatis omnino plantis, (quod et similiter facere debent omnes, tam ministri altaris, quam alii Fratres adoraturi) procedat versus Crucem, hoc ordine, et successionis distantia, ut in prædicta extremitate spatii devote genua flectat, caputque inclinet; et mox surgat, ac gravi incessu usque ad medium ejusdem spatii pergens, illic denuo genua flectat, et caput inclinet; denique inde procedens, ad pedes Crucis se toto corpore prosternat, eosdem semel dumtaxat deosculetur, moram non trahens; sed mox inde surgens, et facta profunda inclinatione, collocet se ad latus dexterum Crucis, in loco alterius duorum Sacerdotum, dexterum Crucis brachium tenens, quousque ambo Sacerdotes simili ordine et modo adoraverint : primo eorum, postquam adoraverit, se substituente in locum secundi, et pro eo sinistrum Crucis brachium tenente; qui secundus Sacerdos, postquam adoraverit, succedet in locum Prioris a parte dextera, Crucis brachium tenens, una cum alio Sacerdote manente in altero latere, Crucem ad osculum omnium Fratrum adorantium, usque ad finem adorationis, coaptando.
 = Then the public adoration of the holy Cross is made by all the ministers and brethren; in its rite or order an ordered distance of approach is kept between those adoring and those about to adore, in this way: from the place where the Cross lies, a space of about fifteen paces is measured down towards the nave of the church (as the place and its layout allow), and from the farthest end of this space the Prior first, with his feet entirely bare (as all must likewise do, both the ministers of the altar and the other brethren who are to adore), goes towards the Cross in this order and spacing: at the said end of the space he kneels devoutly and bows his head; then rises and, walking with grave step to the middle of the same space, kneels there again and bows his head; finally, going on from there, he prostrates himself full length at the feet of the Cross and kisses them once only, without lingering; then at once rising and bowing profoundly, he places himself at the right side of the Cross, in the place of one of the two priests, holding the right arm of the Cross, until both priests have adored in the same order and manner: the first of them, after adoring, taking the place of the second and holding the left arm of the Cross for him; and the second priest, after adoring, succeeding to the Prior's place on the right side, holding the arm of the Cross, together with the other priest remaining on the other side, presenting the Cross to the kiss of all the brethren who adore, until the end of the adoration.
R: Hæc porro adoratio sequenti ordine perfici debet : post duos Sacerdotes adoret Diaconus pro Missa notatus; postea Diaconi, qui cantaverunt *Agios,* unus post alterum, (et hi, postquam adoraverint, in sacristiam secedant, sacras vestes deposituri) : post Diaconos sequatur Subdiaconus pro Missa notatus; deinde Acolythi, et demum post ministros ceteri Fratres secundum ordinem graduum suorum, incipiendo a majoribus, alternatim procedentes ab ultima spatii extremitate versus Crucem, eo ritu, ordine, ac successionis distantia, quo de Priore supra dictum est, illam adorent, et deosculentur, ac post osculum mox surgendo, facta inclinatione profunda, ad chorum in loca sua revertantur. Illa autem Fratrum successio cum tali intercapedine fiat, quod dum primus surgit a loco primæ suæ genuflexionis, statim illic subintret alter ei proximus, et consequenter alii, servato eodem tenore interstitiorum. Dum ita Crux adoratur, Chorus cantet Antiphonas sequentes, et Hymn. *Crux fidélis.* Prior autem, et ministri in presbyterio sessum eant, ibique easdem Antiphonas, et Hymnum recitent.
 = This adoration is to be carried out in the following order: after the two priests the Deacon appointed for the Mass adores; then the deacons who sang the *Agios,* one after the other (and these, after adoring, withdraw to the sacristy to put off the sacred vestments); after the deacons follows the Subdeacon appointed for the Mass; then the Acolytes; and finally, after the ministers, the other brethren according to the order of their rank, beginning with the seniors, coming alternately from the far end of the space towards the Cross, adore and kiss it with the same rite, order and spacing as was said above of the Prior, and after the kiss, rising at once and bowing profoundly, return to their places in choir. The brethren follow one another at such an interval that, as the first rises from the place of his first genuflection, the next at once takes his place there, and so the others, keeping the same spacing. While the Cross is thus adored, the Choir sings the following Antiphons and the hymn *Crux fidélis.* The Prior and the ministers sit in the presbytery and there recite the same Antiphons and Hymn.
T: {Aña} Tuam Crucem adorámus Dómine, tuam gloriósam recólimus passiónem : miserére nostri, qui passus es pro nobis.
 = {Ant.} We adore Your Cross, O Lord, we commemorate Your glorious passion; have mercy on us, You who suffered for us.
C: ad 2
T: {Aña} Crucem tuam adorámus Dómine, et sanctam resurrectiónem tuam laudámus, et glorificámus : ecce enim propter Crucem venit gáudium in univérso mundo.
 = {Ant.} We adore Your Cross, O Lord, and we praise and glorify Your holy resurrection; for behold, by the Cross joy has come into the whole world.
C: ad 3
T: {Aña} Adorémus Crucis signáculum, per quod salútis súmpsimus sacraméntum.
 = {Ant.} Let us adore the sign of the Cross, through which we have received the sacrament of salvation.
C: ad 4
L: Hymnus
 = Hymn
T: ^CRUX fidélis, inter omnes arbor una nóbilis : nulla silva talem profert fronde, flore, gérmine : dulce lignum, dulces clavos, dulce pondus sústinet. {℣.} Pange lingua gloriósi prǽlium certáminis : et super Crucis trophǽum dic triúmphum nóbilem : quáliter Redémptor orbis immolátus vícerit. {℣.} De paréntis protoplásti fraude Factor cóndolens, quando pomi noxiális morsu in mortem córruit, ipse lignum tunc notávit, damna ligni ut sólveret. {℣.} Hoc opus nostræ salútis ordo depopóscerat, multifórmis proditóris ars ut artem fálleret : et medélam ferret inde, hostis unde lǽserat. {℣.} Quando venit ergo sacri plenitúdo témporis, missus est ab arce Patris Natus, orbis Cónditor : ac de ventre virgináli caro factus pródiit. {℣.} Vagit infans inter arcta cónditus præsépia : membra pannis involúta Virgo Mater álligat, et manus, pedésque, et crura, stricta cingit fáscia. {℣.} Lustris sex qui jam peráctis, tempus implens córporis, se volénte natus ad hoc, passióni déditus, Agnus in Crucis levátur immolándus stípite. {℣.} Hic acétum, fel, arúndo, spina, clavi, láncea : mite corpus perforátur, sanguis, unda prófluit : terra, pontus, astra, mundus, quo lavántur flúmine. {℣.} Flecte ramos arbor alta, tensa laxa víscera : et rigor lentéscat ille, quem dedit natívitas : et supérni membra Regis miti tendas stípite. {℣.} Sola digna tu fuísti ferre sǽcli prétium, atque portum præparáre nauta mundo náufrago : quem sacer cruor perúnxit, fusus Agni córpore. {℣.} Glória et honor Deo usquequáque altíssimo, una Patri, Filióque, ínclyto Paráclito : cui laus est, et potéstas per ætérna sǽcula. Amen.
 = Faithful Cross, the one noble tree among all; no forest yields its like in leaf, in flower, in fruit: sweet the wood, sweet the nails, sweet the weight it bears. {℣.} Sing, my tongue, the battle of the glorious struggle, and over the trophy of the Cross proclaim the noble triumph: how the world's Redeemer, being sacrificed, conquered. {℣.} Grieving at the deceit of our first parent, when by the bite of the deadly fruit he fell into death, the Maker Himself then marked the tree, to undo the harm of the tree. {℣.} This work the order of our salvation had demanded, that art should outwit the art of the manifold betrayer, and bring the remedy from where the enemy had wounded. {℣.} When therefore the fullness of the sacred time had come, the Son, Maker of the world, was sent from the Father's throne, and came forth from the Virgin's womb made flesh. {℣.} The Infant cries, laid in a narrow manger; the Virgin Mother binds His limbs wrapped in swaddling clothes, and a tight band girds His hands and feet and legs. {℣.} Thirty years now accomplished, completing the time of His bodily life, born for this of His own will, given up to the passion, the Lamb is lifted up on the tree of the Cross to be sacrificed. {℣.} Here are vinegar, gall, the reed, thorns, nails and lance; the gentle Body is pierced, blood and water flow forth: earth, sea, stars and world are washed in that stream. {℣.} Bend your boughs, O lofty tree, relax your tense sinews, and let that stiffness which nature gave you soften, and stretch the limbs of the King on high upon a gentle trunk. {℣.} You alone were worthy to bear the ransom of the world, and as a sailor to prepare a haven for the shipwrecked world, you whom the sacred blood anointed, shed from the Lamb's body. {℣.} Glory and honour to God most high everywhere, alike to the Father, to the Son and to the glorious Paraclete; to whom be praise and power through everlasting ages. Amen.
C: ad 5
R: Si, antequam hæc fuerint omnino cantata, adoratio completa esset, nihilominus integra cantentur; cumque Versus ultimus Hymni dicetur, Prior veniat ad medium gradus presbyterii inter altare et Sacerdotes qui tenent Crucem : ut, terminata adoratione, Sacerdotes Crucem reverenter elevantes, eam Priori stanti super gradus presbyterii tradant, qui ipsam accipiens, et devote erigens, stans verso vultu ad populum, vel Conventum, incipiat Antiphonam
 = If the adoration is finished before all this has been sung, it is nevertheless sung in full; and when the last verse of the hymn is being sung, the Prior comes to the middle of the steps of the presbytery between the altar and the priests holding the Cross, so that, when the adoration is ended, the priests, reverently raising the Cross, hand it to the Prior standing on the steps of the presbytery; and he, receiving it and devoutly raising it, standing facing the people or the Community, begins the Antiphon:
T: Super ómnia ligna cedrórum tu sola excélsior, in qua vita mundi pepéndit, in qua Christus triumphávit, et mors mortem superávit in ætérnum.
 = Above all the cedar trees you alone are higher, on which the life of the world hung, on which Christ triumphed, and death overcame death for ever.
C: ad 6
R: In inchoatione hujus Antiphonæ, tam ministri altaris, et duo Sacerdotes, quam Chorus, genua flectant, inclinantes profunde. Et Cantore prosequente *Ligna cedrórum,* omnes surgant; et Prior ambabus manibus Crucem sustineat elevatam, et Antiphona finita, eodem modo tenens Crucem, dicat sequentem Orationem, sine *Dóminus vobíscum* et sine *Orémus,* Fratribus super formas prostratis.
 = At the beginning of this Antiphon the ministers of the altar, the two priests and the Choir kneel, bowing profoundly; and as the Cantor continues *Ligna cedrórum,* all rise; and the Prior holds the Cross raised with both hands, and when the Antiphon is finished, holding the Cross in the same way, he says the following Oration, without *Dóminus vobíscum* and without *Orémus,* the brethren lying prostrate upon the forms.
L: Oratio
 = Prayer
T: ^RESPICE, quǽsumus Dómine, super hanc famíliam tuam; pro qua Dóminus noster Jesus Christus non dubitávit mánibus tradi nocéntium : et Crucis subíre torméntum.
 = Look down, we beseech You, O Lord, upon this Your family, for which our Lord Jesus Christ did not hesitate to be delivered into the hands of the wicked and to undergo the torment of the Cross.
R: Oratione dicta, Prior Crucem tradat Sacristæ, qui illam statuet in aliquo loco apto, ubi possit ea die a ceteris adorari. Ubi vero propter Fratrum paucitatem, tot Sacerdotes et Diaconi ad præmissa officia distincte obeunda non sunt : Prior cum alio Sacerdote, et Diaconus minister cum alio Diacono prædicta officia exsequantur.
 = The Oration said, the Prior hands the Cross to the Sacristan, who sets it in some suitable place where it may be adored by the others that day. Where, because of the small number of brethren, there are not enough priests and deacons to carry out these offices separately, the Prior with another priest, and the Deacon minister with another deacon, perform them.

INC: Quad6f_d
